# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

menu "Mesh-gate example configuration"

config EXAMPLE_MESH_ID
	string "Mesh ID"
	default "morse-mesh"
	help
	    Must match the mesh nodes this gate serves (see the `mesh` example).

config EXAMPLE_MESH_MAX_PLINKS
	int "Maximum mesh peer links"
	default 16
	range 1 16

config EXAMPLE_RANN_INTERVAL_MS
	int "Gate announcement (RANN) interval, milliseconds"
	default 5000
	help
	    How often the gate floods a root announcement carrying the IS_GATE flag,
	    so mesh nodes discover a way off the mesh.

	    This value goes onto the wire as a raw number with no millisecond-to-TU
	    conversion, so every gate on the same mesh — including a Linux one — must
	    use the same value.

config EXAMPLE_AP_SSID
	string "SoftAP SSID"
	default "MorseMicroESP32AP"

config EXAMPLE_AP_PSK
	string "SoftAP passphrase"
	default "12345678"
	help
	    At least 8 characters — required for SAE.

config EXAMPLE_AP_MAX_STAS
	int "Maximum AP clients"
	default 4
	range 1 16
	help
	    The gate tracks its clients in a fixed table to decide which proxied mesh
	    frames belong to its AP side, so this is bounded on purpose.

	    The upper bound is 16 because the proactive proxy-ARP push costs
	    O(clients x mesh hosts) of airtime in both directions — about 5% at 16
	    clients with 5 mesh nodes on the default refresh interval. Two limits
	    outside this example bind before the table does: MESH_MPP_MAX (32),
	    which lives on every mesh node and which 16 clients on one gate already
	    half-fills, and the flat /24's ~98 usable addresses.

config EXAMPLE_S1G_CHANNEL
	int "S1G channel"
	default 27
	help
	    Both the mesh and the SoftAP run on this one channel — the MM6108 has a
	    single radio, so the two interfaces are necessarily co-channel.

config EXAMPLE_S1G_OPCLASS
	int "S1G operating class"
	default 68

config EXAMPLE_SUBNET_PREFIX
	string "IPv4 /24 prefix shared by the mesh and the AP"
	default "10.9.9."
	help
	    Mesh nodes and AP clients live on ONE flat subnet — that is the point of
	    bridging at layer 2. The gate's AP side is <prefix>1 and hands out DHCP
	    leases from <prefix>2 upwards; mesh nodes take <prefix>100 and above.

config EXAMPLE_PROXY_ARP_PUSH_MS
	int "Proxy-ARP refresh interval, milliseconds (0 disables)"
	default 15000
	help
	    Period at which the gate teaches each host it knows about the hosts on the
	    other side of the bridge, over a reliable unicast.

	    This is UPKEEP only — the gate also pushes immediately whenever it learns a
	    new host, so a joining client is taught in milliseconds rather than waiting
	    out the interval. Shortening this therefore buys very little and costs
	    airtime quadratically: the sweep is O(clients x mesh hosts) in both
	    directions, so at 16 clients and 5 mesh nodes 15 s costs about 5% of
	    airtime and 3 s costs about 26%. Entries live 5 minutes, so 15 s still
	    refreshes each mapping twenty times over its lifetime.

	    Set to 0 to rely on reactive proxy-ARP alone. That still works, but
	    resolution then depends on a broadcast ARP request surviving the crossing,
	    which is unacknowledged and lossy.

endmenu
